WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.475
This paper studies a puzzling training instability
in behavior cloning.

00:00:04.555 --> 00:00:09.287
When you train a policy network with minibatch
stochastic gradient descent,

00:00:09.367 --> 00:00:13.778
the long-horizon reward can swing wildly from one
iterate to the next,

00:00:13.858 --> 00:00:17.050
even though the behavior cloning loss barely moves.

00:00:17.130 --> 00:00:21.733
The authors trace this to what they call gradient
variance amplification,

00:00:21.813 --> 00:00:27.379
where tiny stochastic gradient noise gets amplified
into catastrophic error accumulation

00:00:27.459 --> 00:00:31.421
through the closed loop between the policy and its
environment.

00:00:31.501 --> 00:00:33.474
Most standard fixes do not help,

00:00:33.554 --> 00:00:39.441
but taking an exponential moving average of the
weights turns out to be remarkably effective,

00:00:39.521 --> 00:00:43.290
and the same story holds for autoregressive
language models.
